  <NugetDependency Id="UU8BGxsq638jIjuU8FbOcP" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="UoWWVahnaWBATWijtetuLi">
    <Canvas Id="BtztqHiYGz3J2MjxB9b9Nl" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="KnxOOCJMyw6nSgfbgNYMwA">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="SubwCORzO2BgScS2SJHJeW">
        <Canvas Id="Jatg2dW5qzQDIeB9fLMYsO" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="JujQCUXUj2KYm8dZEcOud6" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1340" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The map is not to scale.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;FIRST, SWITCH THE TILES ON - the toggle marked Enabled, at the top of this patch, next to the&#xD;&#xA;OpenStreetMap URL. It starts off because opening a patch is not the same as agreeing to&#xD;&#xA;network traffic. This one is about where you are, so you want the ground under you.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE CROSS FOLLOWING YOUR MOUSE IS 200 KILOMETRES EACH WAY. Not on the screen: on the GROUND,&#xD;&#xA;north, south, east and west of wherever you are pointing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  DRAG IT NORTH. It swells. DRAG IT SOUTH, towards the equator, and it shrinks. It is the&#xD;&#xA;  same 200 kilometres the whole time. The cross is not changing. The map is.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Take it to Hokkaido, then to Okinawa, then out to Sakhalin. Read STRETCH as you go.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NOW HOLD THE RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON. The cross changes its mind: now it is 2 DEGREES each way&#xD;&#xA;instead of 200 kilometres, and the two arms are visibly UNEQUAL on screen - the north-south&#xD;&#xA;one is longer. Let go and they are equal again.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO DEFINITIONS OF THE SAME SIZE, AND NEITHER LOOKS LIKE THE OTHER. Flick the button and&#xD;&#xA;watch the shape change. Nothing has moved; only the question has.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Button up   equal on the ground   -&gt; square on screen, unequal in degrees&#xD;&#xA;  Button down equal in degrees      -&gt; unequal on screen, unequal on the ground&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  Web Mercator keeps ANGLES and gives up SIZE. That bargain has a price, the price is&#xD;&#xA;  1 / cos(latitude), and STRETCH is that price, live, at your cursor:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    1.00 on the equator      1.23 at Tokyo      2.00 at 60 degrees north&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  and it runs away to infinity at the pole, which is why Greenland ends up the size of&#xD;&#xA;  Africa. Every web map you have ever used is doing this, everywhere, all the time.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  THIS IS WHY YOU CANNOT DRAW A FIVE-KILOMETRE CIRCLE BY BUFFERING IN DEGREES. Tutorial 08&#xD;&#xA;  drew a Buffer with its Distance in degrees and said so in its own caveat: the circle is&#xD;&#xA;  really a slightly tall ellipse. This patch is that caveat, measured. The east-west arm&#xD;&#xA;  needs MORE degrees than the north-south one to cover the same ground, by exactly the&#xD;&#xA;  factor in the STRETCH box.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  The honest fix is a metric coordinate system - a local projected CRS - and nothing in&#xD;&#xA;  this family has one yet. Written down as a gap rather than hidden. What this patch does&#xD;&#xA;  instead is the cheap correct thing: divide the east-west arm by cos(latitude) and stop&#xD;&#xA;  pretending a degree is a distance.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE EACH THING LIVES, because this patch draws in two places at once&#xD;&#xA;  the cross  is anchored to the GROUND. It goes onto the map in longitude and latitude,&#xD;&#xA;             through Feature and FeatureLayer, and the MAP projects it. Pan the map and it&#xD;&#xA;             stays on its patch of earth.&#xD;&#xA;  the text   is anchored to the CURSOR. Furniture, not geography - a Skia layer drawn over&#xD;&#xA;             the map, in the renderer's own space, touching no projection at all.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Every map library in the field is built on that split: Mapsui calls the second one a&#xD;&#xA;  WIDGET and keeps it in a collection separate from its layers, OpenLayers calls it a&#xD;&#xA;  CONTROL, Leaflet ships its scale bar and zoom buttons the same way. A scale bar is not a&#xD;&#xA;  layer, and your cursor is not a feature.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  scale factor    how much a projection stretches the ground at a given place. On Web&#xD;&#xA;                  Mercator it is 1 / cos(latitude), and it is in the box marked STRETCH&#xD;&#xA;  conformal       angles and local shape kept, size not. That is the cross staying square&#xD;&#xA;                  while it grows - a navigator's projection wearing a wall map's clothes&#xD;&#xA;  true distance   distance on the ground, which is not what a projected map measures&#xD;&#xA;  graphic scale   the little bar in the corner of a paper map. On a Mercator web map it is&#xD;&#xA;                  only honest at one latitude, which is why serious ones move as you pan&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  A texture stretched over a sphere has the same problem, and you have solved it before:&#xD;&#xA;  the pixels near the poles of a UV sphere are enormous and nobody thinks the texture is&#xD;&#xA;  wrong. A map is that, run backwards, with somebody's house in it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 10        the same lie, seen whole: three projections, one knob, no map&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 08        where the Buffer in degrees came from, and its caveat&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 05        buffers again, now that you know what their units are worth">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Rq5EfjohrEY1ZmJXclKPJy" Comment="OpenStreetMap" Bounds="-50,-115,430,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png">
